WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.800
Marvel never disappoints when it&nbsp;
comes to cameos and surprise reveals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.440
and the latest could change Thor's story&nbsp;
forever. In fact, the mid-credits scene in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.960
"Thor: Love and Thunder" may have teased&nbsp;
a major battle. Warning! Spoilers ahead.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.680
The "Thor" movies have always tried to&nbsp;
find the humanity in omnipotent gods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.680
Thor Odinson may be worshipped across&nbsp;
the lands, but at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.080
he's really just looking for acceptance&nbsp;
and love from those he holds dear.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.440
Nowhere is that more evident&nbsp;
than in his latest excursion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.440
"Thor: Love and Thunder." The stories&nbsp;
of Tony Stark and Steve Rogers may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.680
have closed their final chapters, but&nbsp;
Thor's saga is still being written.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.080
In between battling aliens and getting into&nbsp;
verbal spars with the Guardians of the Galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.200
Thor's really just looking&nbsp;
for a new purpose. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.080
things didn't exactly end well between&nbsp;
him and his former flame, Jane Foster.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.280
"Sorry to hear that Jane dumped you."

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.640
"She didn't dump me, you know. I&nbsp;
dumped her. It was a mutual dumping."

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.120
When she comes back into his life,&nbsp;
things get complicated quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.880
They'll work together to bring down Gorr&nbsp;
the God Butcher, and in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.320
Thor may just realize that it's better&nbsp;
to love those close to you for the short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.920
time you'll be together than to&nbsp;
go through immortal life alone.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.160
That said, where one battle ends,&nbsp;
another seems just to be beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.360
Throughout his latest adventure,&nbsp;
Thor gets into some trouble in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.880
Omnipotent City when he strikes down Zeus. At&nbsp;
first, it seems like the Greek god is dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.880
but as we see in the mid-credits scene,&nbsp;
he's still very much among the living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.080
And he has some substantial plans to get back&nbsp;
at Thor by sending one of his own after him.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.240
It seems obvious that it would take quite a&nbsp;
bit more than a lightning bolt through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.200
chest to take down a god, especially one so&nbsp;
adept at wielding his own electrical powers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.600
And as the tag reveals, he's still&nbsp;
around, surrounded by his mistresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.240
The scene starts with this tableau featuring&nbsp;
Zeus speaking to someone else off-screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.800
and it's clear he holds a mighty big grudge&nbsp;
against the God of Thunder. Toward the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.320
of his speech, Zeus makes it clear that he wants&nbsp;
an unseen warrior to take Thor down a peg. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.840
at this point that we finally get a look at&nbsp;
who's on the other side of this conversation.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.640
It's revealed that Hercules, played&nbsp;
by "Ted Lasso" star Brett Goldstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.120
is now in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.&nbsp;
He looks determined to make his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.360
proud. It's at this point the screen cuts&nbsp;
to black, and the rest of the credits roll.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.480
It doesn't give away much, but it does imply that&nbsp;
Hercules will get into a confrontation with Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.120
down the road. It wouldn't be out of the question&nbsp;
for this to transpire in a possible "Thor 5,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.360
especially seeing how the very end of the&nbsp;
credits promises that Thor will return.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.760
It seems as though his trip to Omnipotent&nbsp;
City will have wide-ranging consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.040
when it comes to getting on the&nbsp;
wrong side of the Greek pantheon.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.200
Naturally, Hercules in Marvel Comics is&nbsp;
based on the hero from Greek mythology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.560
with a few noteworthy twists. He's been around in&nbsp;
the comics for a while through various iterations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.000
but his formal introduction to mass audiences&nbsp;
came in the 1960s when he was introduced as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.120
rival to Thor. From that point forward, he&nbsp;
became a regular fixture in "Thor" comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.120
occasionally being a thorn in&nbsp;
Thor's side but also being an ally.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.560
Given his introduction in "Thor: Love and&nbsp;
Thunder," it would make the most amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.920
sense for him to follow a similar trajectory in&nbsp;
the MCU. It's possible Hercules will initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.560
function as a rival, but the two will likely&nbsp;
become friends over time and team up to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.200
a much larger threat. And from there,&nbsp;
the sky's the limit with the character.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.000
In the comics, Hercules went on to join the&nbsp;
Avengers. That team may be in a state of flux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.120
at the moment, but it'll inevitably reunite.&nbsp;
And Hercules could certainly factor into that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.080
equation. Hercules has a long history from the&nbsp;
comics to pull from, so it's unlikely this will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.640
be the last time we see him on screen as&nbsp;
a part of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

